yoyo outline "dir sp/a b.rs" and yoyo watch "echo a b" no longer treat the literal quotes as part of their argument. It's committed as 6c1280f2 under "Part of #1009", and build, all tests, clippy with -D warnings and the format check pass.

What was wrong (measured on a414d6ee)
• yoyo outline "dir sp/a b.rs" printed No symbols matching ""dir sp/a b.rs"" found. and exited 0, even though the file has symbols.
• yoyo watch "echo a b" saved the command as "echo a b", quotes included, so the shell would look for a program literally named echo a b.

The fix (src/dispatch_sub.rs, no new mechanism)
• outline: now joins the arguments with plain spaces instead of wrapping spaced ones in quotes, the same way blame already does. /outline never strips quotes, so the added quotes were always searched for literally. Arguments without spaces produce exactly the same input as before.
• watch: follows the rule run already uses (b3a2ed90). Exactly one argument is taken as the whole shell command line, and two or more keep today's quoting, so watch echo "a  b" still saves echo "a  b". That matches procps watch: its --help here says -x passes the command "to exec instead of sh -c", so by default it runs the command through sh -c. I couldn't run watch itself on the runner because it needs a terminal.
• The REPL /outline and /watch are unchanged.

Tests (two new integration tests in tests/integration.rs, using the existing helper that clears API keys)
• Both failed before the fix with exactly the outputs above and pass after it. I checked this by reverting only src/dispatch_sub.rs and restoring it in a single command.
• Cases that should not change are pinned with exact-match asserts: watch cargo test, watch echo "a  b", watch off, a plain outline query, a plain outline path, bare outline (exit 2) and a query with no match (exit 0).
• My first "no quotes in the output" assertion was too broad: /outline already puts quotes around the file name in its summary line (in "dir sp/a b.rs"). The assertion now checks for the doubled quotes that were the actual bug.

Docs and follow-up
• docs/src/usage/commands.md now has one sentence on each verb's single-argument behaviour.
• I posted the two fixed cases, the commit and what's left on #1009. docs, lint and skill still show the added quotes inside their refusal messages.
• I amended the commit once before posting. I had quoted the watch man page without reading it (man printed nothing here), so the commit now quotes the --help text I actually saw.
